1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:03,740
Imagine you are drifting along a
rocky reef in the cold Pacific.

2
00:00:04,490 --> 00:00:06,040
You have done your research.

3
00:00:06,790 --> 00:00:08,120
Sharks are rare here.

4
00:00:08,970 --> 00:00:10,580
Seals ignore divers.

5
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:13,270
Nothing in this water is hunting you.

6
00:00:14,090 --> 00:00:15,130
That is the rule.

7
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:16,980
Then you feel it.

8
00:00:17,770 --> 00:00:22,200
Something soft and cold slides across your
ankle, and it does not feel like kelp.

9
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:25,940
It pulls gently, as if testing the grip.

10
00:00:26,820 --> 00:00:31,190
That is a giant Pacific octopus, and
it already knows exactly what you are.

11
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:34,830
When most people picture
a dangerous sea creature,

12
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:36,860
they picture a great white or an orca.

13
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:41,370
But the giant Pacific octopus
belongs to a very different category.

14
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:45,560
It has no bones, no shell, and no fins.

15
00:00:46,310 --> 00:00:48,780
Yet it is the largest octopus on earth,

16
00:00:48,630 --> 00:00:52,950
it hunts sharks, and it can disappear from your
sight while you are looking straight at it.

17
00:00:53,730 --> 00:00:56,980
So let's talk about why you would
never want to meet one underwater.

18
00:00:57,930 --> 00:01:01,050
First, the size, because
it is hard to imagine.

19
00:01:01,990 --> 00:01:05,500
A typical adult weighs
around 15 to 30 kilograms,

20
00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:08,540
and its arms can reach more
than 3 meters from tip to tip.

21
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:11,930
Biggest ones are reported
to be even larger.

22
00:01:12,770 --> 00:01:15,400
Picture a creature as heavy as a big dog,

23
00:01:15,250 --> 00:01:17,850
with eight arms longer
than a grown man is tall.

24
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:20,440
And all of that is soft muscle.

25
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:23,450
There is not a single bone inside it.

26
00:01:24,230 --> 00:01:26,170
That one fact changes everything.

27
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:30,650
An octopus can squeeze through
any opening larger than its beak,

28
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:32,600
which is the only hard part of its body.

29
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:35,700
The size of a coin is enough.

30
00:01:36,610 --> 00:01:40,520
A creature this heavy can pour itself
through a crack in the rocks like water,

31
00:01:40,370 --> 00:01:41,780
and you would never see it go.

32
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:44,590
Now let's look at what it eats,

33
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:46,870
because that is where this
animal gets uncomfortable.

34
00:01:47,750 --> 00:01:52,120
Crabs, clams, snails, and
lobsters are its daily meals.

35
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:54,330
It also eats fish.

36
00:01:55,080 --> 00:01:56,440
And it eats sharks.

37
00:01:57,160 --> 00:02:02,350
A giant Pacific octopus was filmed at the
Seattle Aquarium catching a dogfish shark,

38
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:04,660
wrapping it in its
arms, and pulling it in.

39
00:02:05,510 --> 00:02:09,940
Divers in Puget Sound have also found
shark remains near octopus dens.

40
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:13,530
And just like any good
hunter, it keeps a record.

41
00:02:14,310 --> 00:02:18,940
Outside its den, an octopus leaves
a pile of empty shells and claws,

42
00:02:18,790 --> 00:02:21,080
a receipt for every meal it has ever had.

43
00:02:21,890 --> 00:02:24,950
Most of us cannot find a single
receipt when we need one.

44
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:28,950
So how does it find all this
food in dark, cold water?

45
00:02:29,770 --> 00:02:30,780
Not with sound.

46
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:35,500
Each arm is covered in suckers,

47
00:02:35,350 --> 00:02:38,520
and a giant Pacific octopus
has more than 2,000 of them.

48
00:02:39,490 --> 00:02:41,560
But these are not plain suction cups.

49
00:02:42,310 --> 00:02:44,310
Every sucker can taste and feel.

50
00:02:45,130 --> 00:02:47,060
When an arm slides across a rock,

51
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:51,030
The octopus is reading it, like
fingertips that can also taste.

52
00:02:51,750 --> 00:02:55,290
It feels the crab hiding in the
crack long before it ever sees it.

53
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:59,380
Next comes the part that makes
the octopus truly unsettling.

54
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:01,340
It can disappear.

55
00:03:02,150 --> 00:03:05,500
Beneath its skin sit
millions of tiny color cells,

56
00:03:05,350 --> 00:03:07,670
and the octopus controls
every one of them.

57
00:03:08,490 --> 00:03:10,880
In less than a second it can match sand,

58
00:03:10,730 --> 00:03:15,420
seaweed, or rough stone, and even raise
bumps to copy the texture around it.

59
00:03:16,290 --> 00:03:17,690
Here is the strange part.

60
00:03:18,440 --> 00:03:22,900
Its eyes do not see color the way ours
do, yet it matches colors perfectly.

61
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:25,660
So you are not searching for an animal.

62
00:03:26,570 --> 00:03:29,660
You are searching for a rock that
might be blinking back at you.

63
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:32,440
But it is not only camouflage.

64
00:03:33,130 --> 00:03:34,460
It is also smart.

65
00:03:35,210 --> 00:03:37,440
An octopus has a central brain,

66
00:03:37,290 --> 00:03:42,140
but most of its neurons, hundreds of millions
in total, are spread through its arms.

67
00:03:42,850 --> 00:03:46,870
Each arm can react and make small
decisions without waiting for permission.

68
00:03:47,720 --> 00:03:51,480
So when an arm touches your fin,
you are not dealing with one mind.

69
00:03:52,390 --> 00:03:53,850
You are dealing with nine.

70
00:03:54,730 --> 00:03:57,040
And it has three hearts and blue blood,

71
00:03:56,890 --> 00:03:59,960
because it carries oxygen
using copper instead of iron.

72
00:04:00,870 --> 00:04:03,770
That works well in
freezing, low-oxygen water.

73
00:04:04,650 --> 00:04:07,030
These animals also solve problems.

74
00:04:07,780 --> 00:04:11,640
They open jars from the inside and
can tell individual people apart.

75
00:04:12,390 --> 00:04:16,780
In a German aquarium in the
1950s, an octopus named Otto kept

76
00:04:16,630 --> 00:04:20,660
squirting water at a bright lamp until
it short-circuited and blew the lights.

77
00:04:21,450 --> 00:04:22,810
That was not an accident.

78
00:04:23,720 --> 00:04:24,700
That was a plan.

79
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:28,780
All of this raises an obvious question.

80
00:04:29,630 --> 00:04:32,660
Has an octopus ever turned
all that power on humans?

81
00:04:33,370 --> 00:04:35,790
Not in any way that matches the legends.

82
00:04:36,570 --> 00:04:37,680
For centuries,

83
00:04:37,530 --> 00:04:41,740
sailors told stories of tentacled
monsters dragging ships under the waves.

84
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:47,060
Most historians think those stories came
from giant squid or pure imagination.

85
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:50,130
But an animal that can hide in plain sight

86
00:04:49,980 --> 00:04:52,980
and grip with eight arms
makes a perfect monster story.

87
00:04:53,790 --> 00:04:55,540
Now let's say the worst happened.

88
00:04:56,410 --> 00:04:58,540
An octopus decided you were a problem.

89
00:04:59,390 --> 00:05:00,690
What would you actually do?

90
00:05:01,790 --> 00:05:02,700
Break it down.

91
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:04,750
You could swim away.

92
00:05:05,530 --> 00:05:07,920
But an octopus can fire a jet of water

93
00:05:07,770 --> 00:05:10,380
out of its body and shoot
backward in an instant.

94
00:05:11,170 --> 00:05:15,150
And you would be swimming with a heavy
tank and flippers, tired and cold.

95
00:05:15,970 --> 00:05:19,150
That is like trying to outrun
something that does not need legs.

96
00:05:19,900 --> 00:05:21,460
You could try to dive deeper.

97
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:25,250
But giant Pacific octopuses
live from the shallows

98
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:27,180
all the way to hundreds of meters down.

99
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:29,650
Going deeper is not hiding.

100
00:05:30,460 --> 00:05:32,210
That is walking into its kitchen.

101
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:34,740
You could stay perfectly still.

102
00:05:35,550 --> 00:05:37,970
But the octopus does not need you to move.

103
00:05:38,690 --> 00:05:42,190
It can feel the water move, and
it can taste you with every touch.

104
00:05:42,940 --> 00:05:44,430
You could try to pull free.

105
00:05:45,340 --> 00:05:47,980
The more you pull, the more arms join in.

106
00:05:48,770 --> 00:05:52,370
And if you scare it, it will probably
release a cloud of black ink.

107
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:54,990
The ink does more than hide it.

108
00:05:55,870 --> 00:05:58,380
It dulls the sense of
smell of anything nearby.

109
00:05:59,170 --> 00:06:01,080
Now you are alone in the dark,

110
00:06:00,930 --> 00:06:03,790
with an animal that knows
the area far better than you.

111
00:06:04,640 --> 00:06:06,510
So the honest verdict is this.

112
00:06:07,260 --> 00:06:09,710
If an octopus ever decided
you were a problem,

113
00:06:09,890 --> 00:06:11,500
there would not be much you could do.

114
00:06:12,290 --> 00:06:14,160
But here is the surprising part.

115
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:16,370
It almost never does.

116
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:20,530
There is no pattern of giant
Pacific octopuses hunting people.

117
00:06:21,370 --> 00:06:25,170
Divers in Puget Sound and off British
Columbia meet them all the time.

118
00:06:25,980 --> 00:06:30,510
Usually the octopus changes color,
backs into its den, and watches.

119
00:06:31,290 --> 00:06:33,970
Sometimes it gently feels a diver's glove.

120
00:06:35,130 --> 00:06:36,940
It can happen, but it is rare.

121
00:06:37,790 --> 00:06:41,070
To an octopus, you are a
strange, bubbling visitor.

122
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:44,500
It wants you gone about as
much as you want a fly gone.

123
00:06:45,310 --> 00:06:47,890
But the real danger has
always gone the other way.

124
00:06:48,670 --> 00:06:52,430
A giant Pacific octopus
lives only 3 to 5 years.

125
00:06:53,180 --> 00:06:55,950
After mating, the male weakens and dies.

126
00:06:56,700 --> 00:06:59,090
The female lays tens of thousands of eggs

127
00:06:58,940 --> 00:07:01,300
in a hidden den and
guards them for months.

128
00:07:02,050 --> 00:07:03,250
She does not leave.

129
00:07:04,060 --> 00:07:05,170
She does not eat.

130
00:07:05,950 --> 00:07:08,780
She cleans the eggs and
blows fresh water over them.

131
00:07:09,630 --> 00:07:12,460
When the eggs finally hatch, she dies too.

132
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:16,530
This huge, intelligent hunter
never meets her children.

133
00:07:17,340 --> 00:07:18,740
And the threats are growing.

134
00:07:19,550 --> 00:07:22,900
Octopuses get caught in
fishing gear and crab traps,

135
00:07:22,750 --> 00:07:26,540
and sudden ocean heat waves can
disturb the cold water they depend on.

136
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:29,990
It is easy to call the octopus a monster,

137
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:32,400
but this animal holds its
part of the ocean together.

138
00:07:33,210 --> 00:07:35,920
It eats huge numbers of crabs and shrimp,

139
00:07:35,770 --> 00:07:39,730
and it is also food for sea
otters, seals, and sharks.

140
00:07:40,450 --> 00:07:43,630
It sits in the middle of the
food web, keeping the balance.

141
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:47,630
We tell ourselves nothing in the
sea is really looking for us.

142
00:07:48,380 --> 00:07:50,700
And honestly, that is mostly true.

143
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:54,190
But somewhere out there,
in the cold green dark.

144
00:07:54,370 --> 00:07:56,120
Something with nine brains,

145
00:07:55,970 --> 00:07:59,340
three hearts, and two thousand
tasting fingers is watching.

146
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:02,350
It just has better things to do than us.

147
00:08:03,170 --> 00:08:04,180
Thanks for watching.

148
00:08:04,990 --> 00:08:08,850
If there is a sea creature you want
to see next, tell me in the comments.

149
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:10,580
I do read them.

